 ### [MIMO-DONeRF](16217-mimo-donerf.md)
-MIMO-DONeRF applies the Multi-Input Multi-Output (MIMO) concept to the DONeRF architecture [16217-mimo-donerf.md]. It modifies the shading network to accept multiple spatial inputs and view directions simultaneously, producing multiple color and density outputs per forward pass. This improves inference speed compared to increasing the number of samples in standard DONeRF, while maintaining or improving image quality by allowing the network to accumulate neighbor information through grouped samples. It differs from standard DONeRF by processing $N_p$ samples in parallel rather than sequentially, and from MIMO-NeRF by being specifically adapted to the two-network structure (depth oracle and shading) of DONeRF.
+MIMO-DONeRF is a method that applies the Multi-Input Multi-Output (MIMO) neural rendering concept to the DONeRF architecture. It modifies the shading network to accept multiple spatial inputs and view directions simultaneously, producing multiple color and density outputs per forward pass. This approach improves inference speed compared to increasing the number of samples in standard DONeRF, while maintaining or improving image quality by allowing the network to accumulate neighbor information through grouped samples. It differs from standard DONeRF by processing $N_p$ samples in parallel rather than sequentially, and from MIMO-NeRF by being specifically adapted to the two-network structure (depth oracle and shading) of DONeRF.
 
 ### [Point-NeRF Adaptation for Object Manipulation](16395-point-nerf-adaptation-for-object-manipulation.md)
-This method adapts the Point-NeRF architecture to enable 3D scene geometry decomposition and manipulation from 2D images [16395-point-nerf-adaptation-for-object-manipulation.md]. It solves the problem of editing specific objects within a complex scene by replacing the standard Multi-View Stereo (MVS) point cloud generation with ground-truth dense data during training and using a diffusion model to infer object codes for manipulation. It differs from standard Point-NeRF by integrating a DM-NeRF module to map spatial locations to object codes and modifying the rendering pipeline to support point transformation.
